WEBVTT

00:00:00.453 --> 00:00:05.817
A camera sees a robot, moving shadows, a door, and a changing display.

00:00:05.931 --> 00:00:09.341
Which features should a control representation retain?

00:00:09.454 --> 00:00:11.623
Keeping every pixel is expensive.

00:00:11.736 --> 00:00:20.413
Discarding every uncontrollable variable is also wrong: the robot may not control the wind, but wind can determine which actions move it

00:00:20.526 --> 00:00:21.552
toward a target.

00:00:21.835 --> 00:00:24.090
Use three conceptual components.

00:00:24.203 --> 00:00:27.245
One changes in response to the agent's actions.

00:00:27.358 --> 00:00:32.117
A second evolves on its own but influences the first component's dynamics.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:35.832
A third evolves independently and has no effect on control.

00:00:35.945 --> 00:00:39.853
The displayed factorization makes those distinctions precise.

00:00:39.966 --> 00:00:46.094
It is a model assumption about dynamics, not a label we can assign from visual appearance alone.

00:00:46.377 --> 00:00:52.973
Learning to Perceive the World Through Control studies representations induced by empowerment-based skill learning.

00:00:53.087 --> 00:01:00.806
Its key question is whether those representations can ignore control-irrelevant variation while retaining the features needed to

00:01:00.919 --> 00:01:03.018
distinguish what the agent can do.

00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:09.614
Our wind-and-display example makes the relevant distinction before we examine the mathematical assumptions.

00:01:10.917 --> 00:01:16.313
Start with the skill decoder from earlier lessons, but factor it through two encoders.

00:01:16.427 --> 00:01:20.889
One represents the initial state and the other represents the future state.

00:01:21.002 --> 00:01:25.962
Together they retain the information needed to infer which skill produced the outcome.

00:01:26.076 --> 00:01:29.336
These are called forward and backward representations.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:32.006
Bayes rule makes their target explicit.

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:39.426
Multiply the skill prior by the probability that the skill reaches the observed outcome, then normalize across skills.

00:01:39.539 --> 00:01:45.601
A feature matters to this posterior only if it changes the relative evidence for skill identities.

00:01:45.714 --> 00:01:51.172
This creates a representation-learning signal without supplying a downstream task label.

00:01:51.455 --> 00:01:53.651
There is a crucial qualification.

00:01:53.764 --> 00:02:00.891
A sufficiently large network could predict the correct posterior while also storing arbitrary irrelevant details.

00:02:01.004 --> 00:02:09.543
The theory therefore studies minimal sufficient representations: representations that preserve exactly the distinctions needed by the

00:02:09.657 --> 00:02:10.482
posterior.

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:18.803
Correct prediction alone is not the same as compression, and a practical neural network is not guaranteed to satisfy minimality.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:26.160
Think of the forward representation as describing the control possibilities available from a state.

00:02:26.273 --> 00:02:31.417
Two versions of a machine might use different button names while offering the same effects.

00:02:31.531 --> 00:02:39.671
If actions can be matched by their transition distributions, the superficial interface labels should not define different underlying

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:40.835
capabilities.

00:02:41.118 --> 00:02:48.403
In our diagram, one panel maps the upper button to opening a gate, while another swaps the physical labels.

00:02:48.516 --> 00:02:52.437
The useful comparison pairs actions by their consequences.

00:02:52.551 --> 00:03:00.809
A controller still needs to issue the correct button in each interface, but a representation of the available control structure can

00:03:00.923 --> 00:03:04.115
identify the equivalent starting situations.

00:03:04.399 --> 00:03:10.267
The paper formalizes such equivalences under its policy and representation assumptions.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.959
The point is not that two states with similar pictures must have the same embedding.

00:03:15.073 --> 00:03:21.463
It is that a specific relation between their available transition distributions can justify aliasing.

00:03:21.576 --> 00:03:27.876
Changing a real obstacle, an actuator limit, or the effect of an action may break that relation.

00:03:29.179 --> 00:03:36.837
The backward representation answers a different question: what does observing this outcome tell us about the skill that brought us here?

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:42.611
Suppose two outcomes occur in the same proportion under every skill from a given starting state.

00:03:42.724 --> 00:03:49.800
Their likelihoods differ by a shared factor, so that factor cancels when computing the posterior over skills.

00:03:50.084 --> 00:03:58.197
Under the corresponding conditions across starting states, a minimal backward representation can identify these outcomes.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.512
Yet their future control possibilities might differ.

00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:08.607
Arriving at two locations by indistinguishable routes does not imply that the same exits will be available afterward.

00:04:08.721 --> 00:04:12.612
Incoming evidence and outgoing control are separate structures.

00:04:12.895 --> 00:04:18.327
This is why forcing the two encoders to be identical can discard useful distinctions.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:26.317
In the companion, compare small tables of skill-conditioned probabilities and directly compute the posterior for each outcome.

00:04:26.431 --> 00:04:34.053
You can see when columns carry the same evidence even though we assign the resulting states different future options in the toy model.

00:04:35.356 --> 00:04:39.148
We can see the core cancellation in a simple probability model.

00:04:39.261 --> 00:04:43.976
Let the outcome contain a controllable coordinate and an independent nuisance.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:52.191
If the nuisance has the same distribution for every skill, its probability appears in both the numerator and denominator of Bayes rule.

00:04:52.304 --> 00:04:56.392
It cancels, leaving the same posterior without the nuisance.

00:04:56.675 --> 00:05:02.278
That calculation explains why nuisance variation need not help skill classification.

00:05:02.391 --> 00:05:06.269
It does not prove that any finite-data neural network will ignore it.

00:05:06.382 --> 00:05:15.430
A network might memorize correlations, a policy might condition unnecessarily on the nuisance, or a restricted skill set might fail to

00:05:15.544 --> 00:05:18.434
expose important controllable distinctions.

00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:28.256
The paper's invariance result adds explicit conditions, including its structured dynamics, optimal-posterior analysis, and minimality

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:31.445
assumptions for policies and representations.

00:05:31.558 --> 00:05:32.964
Those conditions matter.

00:05:33.077 --> 00:05:41.507
In our lab, independent duplicate observation channels leave information unchanged, while action-dependent changes can alter it.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:47.920
This isolates one algebraic mechanism without presenting the toy calculation as the full theorem.

00:05:49.223 --> 00:05:54.509
A good representation experiment needs both invariance and sensitivity tests.

00:05:54.622 --> 00:06:01.994
Vary an irrelevant display while holding the control problem fixed: the representation should ideally stay stable.

00:06:02.108 --> 00:06:10.342
Then change a door's availability or an actuator's effect: the representation should preserve the distinction if it changes the relevant

00:06:10.455 --> 00:06:11.669
skill posterior.

00:06:11.952 --> 00:06:14.863
Also test the policy and decoder together.

00:06:14.976 --> 00:06:23.155
An encoder that ignores an important variable can still look compact, and a highly accurate classifier can still store irrelevant

00:06:23.269 --> 00:06:24.160
information.

00:06:24.273 --> 00:06:30.535
Compression, predictive sufficiency, and downstream control are related but different properties.

00:06:30.649 --> 00:06:35.477
Treating any one metric as a complete answer hides the assumptions behind the theory.

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:42.629
The lesson is that empowerment-based interaction offers a principled route toward control-centered representations.

00:06:42.742 --> 00:06:48.896
It does not remove the need to specify what counts as relevant control or to verify the learned model.

00:06:49.009 --> 00:06:57.752
Next we will change the subject of empowerment: instead of expanding the robot's own choices, we will ask the robot to expand a human's

00:06:57.865 --> 00:06:59.150
effective control.
